Subject: [PATCH] url: a Windows profile parses file paths as Windows Chrome does

Chrome on Windows turns things that look like Windows paths into file:
URLs (url_util.cc DoCanonicalize and the WIN32 branches it cites):
new URL("c:/foo") is file:///C:/foo there and a URL with the scheme "c"
everywhere else, and UNC paths, "Z|" drive separators and drive letters
against a file base follow. A page reads the difference with one
.protocol. FingerprintJS does, right after enumerating navigator: on
the same Linux host, stock Chrome, this build under a Windows profile
and a competing build under Linux all read "c:" and score anomaly 1,
and the competing build under Windows reads "file:" and scores 0.

Every WIN32 branch in url/ is now compiled on every host and gated on
url::UsesWindowsFileSemantics() (url/url_file_semantics.h). That reads
one relaxed atomic: true on Windows, false elsewhere until
ChromeMainDelegate::PreSandboxStartup sets it from the installed
profile, in every process type (a Linux zygote before it forks). url/
never calls the profile loader, so the order in which early startup
parses URLs cannot decide the identity, and url, net and content unit
tests keep the host's rules. Callers still test the input's shape
first, so ordinary URLs never read the flag. Windows builds keep
upstream behaviour.

